02:34.500 --> 02:40.500
What's really cool about what's
going on with Gmail is that
email is an API.

02:40.500 --> 02:43.500
It's a very simple one.

02:43.500 --> 02:45.500
It allows you to...

02:45.500 --> 02:53.500
It's basically a flat directory
of documents with enclosures.

02:53.500 --> 03:00.500
The body of the message and the
title of the message can serve
as metadata.

03:00.500 --> 03:07.500
So can the sender of the message
could be thought of as the
creator of the document.

03:07.500 --> 03:10.500
Unix has a driver structure.

03:10.500 --> 03:20.500
On the one hand, email either
simply or pop is a good API.

03:20.500 --> 03:26.500
Unix has an API for the file
system and in fact, so does
Windows.

03:27.000 --> 03:49.000
And it allows you to put in
drivers that basically do for
the operating system the basic
operations of creating a file,
deleting a file, adding some
text to the file, reading some
text from a file or data from a
file.

03:49.000 --> 03:54.000
And there are a few more
operations, but not that many.

03:54.000 --> 04:04.000
Also operations that change the
metadata, the data associated
with files, the creation date,
modification date.

04:04.000 --> 04:07.000
And the Macintosh you have a
resource for.

04:07.000 --> 04:12.000
I'm not sure if they have that
anymore actually with OS 10
being a unit file system.

04:12.000 --> 04:21.000
So in any case, Google didn't do
anything new. They just
supported an existing API that's
very broadly understood and it's
called Electronic Mail.

04:21.000 --> 04:27.000
And that's the beauty of these
formats and protocols.

04:27.000 --> 04:28.000
They make.

04:28.500 --> 04:34.500
both kinds of things possible.
And the lowest common
denominator, I mean Adam, you
would

04:34.500 --> 04:43.500
absolutely, I think you would
love, you know, how simple these
protocols are. They are,

04:43.500 --> 04:46.500
they're all texts for us.

